@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+UTS native-view batch board acceptance
+=====================================
+
+This checklist is for a newly flashed target board. Compile success and clean
+patch replay are prerequisites, not substitutes for any runtime check below.
+Use disposable instance data and record the kernel config, patch digests,
+launcher version, image fingerprint, Run generation and actual namespace IDs.
+Do not use a broad command-line pgrep that may select the diagnostic SSH shell.
+
+Preflight and launch
+--------------------
+
+* Query NS_UTS_GET_INTERFACES once. Verify ABI 1, size 64, zero reserved words,
+  expected class bits and FUSE/input/block/GPU flags for the installed config.
+* Bind explicit objects, not host directory trees. For non-device classes
+  (rfkill, thermal, power_supply), pass class/name and zero major/minor.
+  Update the launcher first: an older DRM/video-only device mapper will not
+  automatically select the new classes.
+* Confirm the launcher authorizes the corresponding char/block nodes through
+  its device cgroup and supplies the intended Android ownership/SELinux labels.
+  Sysfs/event binding alone is not device access authorization.
+* Mount the configured native proc/sysfs, preserve delegated cgroup/selinuxfs
+  roots, mount the private fusectl, and use the launcher's strict recorded-mount
+  checks. Do not disable its monitor to make a boot pass.
+
+Single-instance coverage
+-------------------------
+
+* Hardware discovery uses the selected real device identity and path skeletons,
+  not a simulated GPU. Check the complete platform OF_FULLNAME, OF_COMPATIBLE_*
+  and MODALIAS against the host; on PCI compare the real BDF and identity fields.
+  Check device/subsystem/driver links and card/render grouping, including virtio
+  and its underlying parent when applicable. Ancestor skeletons must not expose
+  unrelated siblings or driver bind/unbind/reset controls.
+* Media controller endpoints may be bus registered: check bus/media/devices and
+  subsystem, without requiring a nonexistent class/media. CAMSS media0 on this
+  board is not the Venus codec. Identify encoder/decoder using QUERYCAP and
+  formats, never by assuming video node numbers.
+* Run a probe linked to the image's libdrm: record drmGetDevices2 before any DRM
+  open, then open and DRM_VERSION separately. A zero enumeration result and a
+  denied open are different failures. Follow with GBM/EGL/Vulkan and real frames;
+  a successful metadata probe does not prove rendering or video streaming.
+* Compare coldboot and real hardware uevent DEVPATH to the projected endpoint.
+  Identity payload must remain real. A private uevent=add must not broadcast an
+  event to the host or another instance. Hardware parent metadata is read-only.
+* Unbinding/rebinding a selected hardware parent's driver must revoke the Run,
+  even if the struct device and dev_t are unchanged. Do this only on disposable
+  hardware: resetting the board's active GPU can affect the host and all users.
+
+* Compare proc meminfo, uptime, CPU IDs, UID CPU/I/O and PSI against the instance
+  limits and elapsed runtime. Check PID-visible proc entries and proc/self/exe.
+  Pseudo-file size zero is normal and does not imply an empty read.
+* Verify proc/partitions and sys/class/block/dev/block contain only selected
+  block objects; compare dev_t, size, read-only status, partition start/number
+  and queue geometry to the selected real devices. Unapproved whole disks must
+  not be exposed by a partition selection. Do not write mounted image loops.
+* Verify input event name/ID/capability bitmaps through sysfs, proc enumeration
+  and device ioctls. Let the image finish its early-init /dev/input cleanup,
+  then coldboot the authorized sysfs uevents and verify event nodes reappear.
+* Check selected media, dma_heap, misc and sound class links and dev/uevent
+  identity. Binderfs and devpts have separate device lifecycles; lack of a
+  conventional sysfs class link for those is not a failed check.
+* Compare selected thermal, power_supply and rfkill read-only values with the
+  real authorized source. Missing source attributes must stay absent. Try
+  writes and verify that no physical state is changed through the projection.
+* Compare the GPU parent driver with the pinned real driver. On SDM845 this
+  permits gpu_config.sh to choose freedreno without a hardcoded fallback.
+  Exercise the actual Mesa/EGL driver, codec and heap allocation paths.
+* With SELinux enforcing, inspect AVCs and projected-path genfs labels, confirm
+  Android boot_completed, and retain SurfaceFlinger/vold tombstones on failure.
+  SwiftShader execmem and generic-sysfs read denials are image-policy issues,
+  not evidence that a kernel API is absent.
+
+FUSE and cross-instance isolation
+---------------------------------
+
+* Use two instances A/B and an independent disposable host FUSE mount. Create
+  a data FUSE connection in each instance; check their fusectl connection sets
+  are disjoint and exclude the host connection. The host administrative tree
+  may see all three. Also mount fusectl after creating a connection to check
+  initial enumeration, not just live additions.
+* Invoke vold abort_fuse in A and verify B and the host mount still work; verify
+  A does not SIGABRT. Recreate A's mount and repeat in B. Never use a valuable
+  host FUSE filesystem as the isolation test fixture.
+* Pass control-file/directory FDs across the domains using a deliberate test
+  harness. Foreign directory iteration, abort and limit reads/writes must fail,
+  including callers with the same numeric root UID.
+* Tear down mounts/connections and instances in different orders, reload the
+  FUSE module only after no mounts remain, and inspect warnings/refcount errors.
+
+Lifecycle and rejection checks
+-------------------------------
+
+* Coldboot every authorized class and verify standard KOBJECT_UEVENT action,
+  devpath, class, optional dev_t/devname and per-instance sequence. A sysfs-only
+  object must not produce a character device 0:0. Check power/rfkill payloads
+  without leaking physical parent paths.
+* Verify unapproved objects and other-instance events are absent. Replay a
+  userspace message with an approved dev_t and confirm it cannot impersonate
+  the pinned kernel object.
+* Remove a disposable pinned device and verify Run invalidation/ENODEV. Reuse
+  its major/minor and confirm the replacement is not authorized. This is an
+  immutable authorization model: replacement requires a new Run, not silent
+  hotplug acceptance.
+* Check descendant namespaces, retained FDs and init/cgroup exit behavior.
+  Stress ordinary task and leaf-cgroup creation/removal without accepting
+  foreign roots or suppressing live-task permission failures.
+
+Known deliberately unprovided interfaces
+----------------------------------------
+
+Host devtmpfs, raw firmware/DT/SoC trees, global ALSA proc/card tables, host
+physical IRQ/buddy/slab lists, global kernel logs and device-mapper control
+are not made private by these patches. Per-instance diskstats accounting and
+arbitrary driver sysfs attributes are not implemented. Metadata/persist storage,
+fstab semantics and Android SELinux policy remain launcher/image contracts.
+Record a concrete consumer and an isolation design before adding any of these.
diff --git a/Documentation/admin-guide/namespaces/uts-views.rst b/Documentation/admin-guide/namespaces/uts-views.rst
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b0a2bd3ed
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/admin-guide/namespaces/uts-views.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,238 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+Native UTS instance views
+=========================
+
+CONFIG_UTS_NS_VIEWS adds an optional host-managed resource domain to native
+procfs and sysfs. It requires UTS resources, PID/net/time namespaces, cgroup
+v2, PSI, and an ARM64 or x86 host. SELinux is independent of this facility.
+Unconfigured UTS namespaces retain ordinary kernel behavior.
+
+Configuration and lifetime
+--------------------------
+
+Only CAP_SYS_ADMIN in the initial user and PID namespaces may configure a
+noninitial UTS namespace. First bind its cgroup with NS_UTS_BIND_RESOURCES,
+then bind its device projection and netns with NS_UTS_BIND_EVENTS, and set
+the time namespace offsets. NS_UTS_CONFIG_VIEW accepts ABI 1, zero flags
+and reserved bytes, the same generation, and PID/net/time namespace FDs.
+The layout is 4304 bytes on native and compat tasks. String fields have
+zero padding and no control characters; cmdline must be nonempty.
+
+The boottime offset must be the negative Run origin. The monotonic offset
+must represent an origin in the same Run, with an elapsed suspend offset
+between zero and the kernel's current suspend offset. These offsets are
+immutable after association, including in descendant time namespaces.
+
+A successful configuration is published once. Reconfiguration returns
+EBUSY; GET_VIEW reports the existing state for recovery. Its 128-byte
+response carries immutable namespace IDs, generation, cgroup, profile
+SHA-256 and an instance boot UUID. BOUND and LIVE are separate bits.
+Capabilities report the implemented interfaces. Initial namespaces may
+not be supplied as instance PID/net/time namespaces. Malformed parameters
+return EINVAL and namespace permission failures return EPERM.
+
+UTS descendants share the resource domain. PID/time descendants inherit
+ownership; guest setns cannot cross resource domains. Namespace objects,
+proc mounts and sysfs superblocks hold references. The domain does not
+hold references to those namespaces, avoiding a netns/event socket cycle.
+The instance init exit, cgroup root removal or disappearance of a pinned
+device revokes LIVE. Subsequent resource access returns ENODEV. Ordinary
+thread and descendant cgroup exit do not revoke the domain.
+
+Mounts and filesystem behavior
+-----------------------------
+
+Mount proc/sysfs only after CONFIG_VIEW. A bootstrap mount created before
+configuration still denotes its original filesystem. The host launcher
+must remove it, and any raw host mount/directory/namespace FDs, before
+running Android. This is a launcher's responsibility; UTS does not revoke
+privileges the host explicitly hands to a guest.
+
+Each proc superblock pins its resource domain and UTS identity; its PID
+namespace must belong to that domain. New and traditional mount APIs
+perform the same check. A retained FD keeps its original source even if
+the reading task enters another namespace. Dynamic seq files retain
+normal read/seek semantics without a userspace cache.
+
+Resource files and raw sysinfo use the same root cgroup accounting.
+Per-CPU user/nice/system counters come from rstat subtree data, including
+removed descendants. Context-switch and fork counters use the same rstat
+propagation. Their scheduler accounting remains disabled until the first
+native view is configured. No physical interrupt is assigned, so IRQ
+counters are zero and IRQ names are absent. Swap, diskstats and buddy device
+lists are empty rather than exposing host-wide accounting. The partitions
+list contains only explicitly authorized, pinned block devices and their
+actual capacity. Slab totals remain
+available in meminfo but physical slab object lists are empty.
+
+VM refault/reclaim counters and a virtual Node 0/Normal zone are supplied
+for LMKD. MemAvailable is the resource ABI's bounded reclaim estimate.
+The VM zone is an instance model, not physical NUMA/DRAM topology. PSI
+read, trigger writes, poll and release share the instance PSI group.
+
+Global proc entries are explicitly classified in fs/proc/native.c.
+Unclassified driver or firmware entries are absent from both lookup and
+readdir. PID/net interfaces retain their ordinary permissions. Generic
+proc chmod/chown changes stay in this superblock rather than rewriting
+global proc_dir_entry metadata. Sysctl permits ordinary network/UTS
+semantics and selected compatible reads. Global writes with no instance
+implementation fail explicitly; they are not accepted as no-ops.
+
+The lock list retains PID-visible POSIX/flock owners. OFD and remote
+locks cannot be assigned to a PID namespace and are omitted from this
+global diagnostic list. Locking syscalls and per-FD lock reports retain
+their original behavior.
+
+The kptr_restrict, dmesg_restrict and perf_event_paranoid controls have
+instance state. Reads and enforcement use the stricter of the instance
+and host values; a guest cannot relax host policy. Bounds and capability
+checks use the original sysctl handlers. The host settings are never
+modified. These controls do not create a private kernel log or PMU.
+
+randomize_va_space, mmap_rnd_bits and mmap_rnd_compat_bits also have
+instance state. ELF loading, stack alignment and mmap layout on ARM64
+and x86 use those values, including compat tasks. Entropy writes retain
+the architecture's actual supported bounds. A successful write affects
+future executable mappings, as the ordinary kernel controls do; it does
+not merely change the displayed proc value.
+
+With ANDROID_UID_CPUTIME and TASK_IO_ACCOUNTING enabled, uid_io/stats
+reports the Android eleven-column UID format: UID, foreground and
+background character/block read/write bytes, then their fsync counts.
+uid_procstat/set accepts a UID and state 0 (foreground) or 1 (background).
+Transitions sample live threads before changing state; exited-thread
+totals remain in the proc PID namespace. UID removal is namespace-local.
+The interfaces retain the standard Android genfs labels. Per-task debug
+rows and per-frequency UID residency interfaces are not supplied.
+
+Sysfs uses a private kernfs root with standard sysfs magic, attributes,
+links, ownership, xattrs and SELinux genfs labeling. CPU IDs follow the
+instance cpuset intersected with online CPUs. Network objects are tagged
+with their actual netns and updated by netdevice notifications. Device
+class/dev-char/dev-block links remain inside the private root. Explicitly
+bound DRM, V4L2, input event, media, DMA heap, misc, sound and block devices
+are pinned by object identity, class, name and device number. Character and
+block device number spaces are distinct. Reusing a device number never
+authorizes the replacement object or its events. Removing a pinned object
+revokes the domain; a new Run is required to authorize its replacement.
+DEVNAME is captured from the pinned source's real uevent, rather than guessed
+from its sysfs name: misc aliases such as mapper/control and standard block
+names retain their real kernel contract. Relative path validation rejects
+absolute names and dot/dot-dot components.
+
+Class numbers 1 and 2 retain their DRM/V4L2 meanings. Numbers 3 through 8
+select input event, media, DMA heap, misc, sound and block devices. Numbers
+9 through 11 select rfkill, thermal and power_supply sysfs objects, which
+have no /dev node: their major/minor fields must both be zero and their
+class/name must match the registered object. Their inclusion does not grant
+access to the global /dev/rfkill control device. That device is independently
+authorized as a misc node. Names may contain commas (for DMA heap names),
+but never slashes or path traversal components. The fixed ABI still supports
+64 entries; unused entries and reserved fields must remain zero.
+
+Input parents provide real name, ID, properties and capability bitmaps.
+/proc/bus/input/devices lists only authorized event handlers. It omits
+physical path/unique strings and unauthorized handlers. Device sysfs files
+and uevent action writes let Android coldboot recreate authorized input nodes
+even if the image removes /dev/input during early initialization.
+
+Block devices provide actual dev, size, read-only status, partition start
+and number, plus read-only queue geometry. /sys/block lists whole disks;
+partition links to a whole disk appear only if both objects are authorized.
+No backing-file paths, unapproved parents, device-mapper control operations
+or host-wide I/O counters are exposed by this projection.
+
+GPU bus selection and PCI IDs needed by drivers are retained. Non-PCI GPU
+parent uevents report the pinned parent's actual bound driver (for example
+msm_dpu), not a synthetic lupine modalias. Host platform addresses and DT
+ancestors are not exported. A parent without a driver reports ENODEV rather
+than pretending to use a particular GPU driver.
+
+Firmware/device-tree, SoC, I2C, regulator and USB trees are not passed through.
+Selected thermal, power_supply and rfkill objects expose a fixed read-only
+attribute allowlist, only where the real source attribute exists. Source
+callbacks are protected by kernfs active references against device removal.
+No store callback is forwarded. Power/rfkill uevents retain only their class
+payload, not physical parent metadata. This is authorized hardware access,
+not per-instance simulation of battery state or temperature. HAL simulation
+is independent and is not represented as a matching sysfs device by this ABI.
+
+Device uevent action writes emit normal NETLINK_KOBJECT_UEVENT messages
+only to the instance netns, using the same mapping as runtime events.
+They do not call the physical device's uevent store. Ordinary socket,
+socketcall and io_uring APIs are unchanged. No private AOSP view protocol
+or filesystem-label exception is required. The image policy must nevertheless
+assign appropriate genfs labels to the projected paths. In particular, an
+Android policy denying surfaceflinger access to generic sysfs files must
+label the private GPU parent uevent appropriately. The kernel does not
+hardcode Android policy type names or grant SELinux permissions.
+
+Interface discovery and /dev ownership
+-------------------------------------
+
+NS_UTS_GET_INTERFACES returns a fixed 64-byte ABI-1 structure with its size,
+a device-class bitmask (bit N is class N), interface flags and zero reserved
+words. This reports compiled support, including configured modules; it is
+not a device authorization, proof that a module is loaded, or board validation.
+Older runtimes can continue using their existing ioctls. New runtimes should
+check support before sealing a Run instead of discovering missing interfaces
+one boot at a time. Unsupported kernels return ENOTTY.
+
+Android /dev remains a private tmpfs populated by its runtime/ueventd, with
+separate binderfs and devpts mounts. The host devtmpfs is not a private device
+namespace and must not be passed through. The runtime still owns device-cgroup
+authorization, mode, ownership and SELinux labeling; binding event/sysfs
+objects does not bypass those controls or create nodes in a mount namespace.
+Non-device sysfs-only selections must not create character device 0:0.
+
+Instance FUSE control
+---------------------
+
+The private sysfs includes /fs/fuse/connections as a mountpoint, even before
+the FUSE module is loaded. A fusectl mount is keyed by the caller's immutable
+resource domain. Each FUSE connection pins the domain in which it was created.
+New and already-existing connections appear only in the matching instance
+tree. Abort/waiting/limit file access and directory iteration reject foreign
+resource-domain callers, even if numeric root UIDs are identical or an open
+descriptor is passed between domains. The host's unbound control tree retains
+its administrative view of all connections.
+
+Connection teardown drops the domain reference from a dedicated workqueue
+after the existing RCU grace period: destroying the final native sysfs view
+may sleep. Module teardown drains the callbacks and that workqueue. A private
+mount must never fall back to the host's control superblock. This facility
+does not change FUSE data mount or request protocols.
+
+Launcher mount inspection must explicitly recognize the instance fusectl
+mount and its ownership; blanket acceptance of extra sysfs mounts is unsafe.
+Images must also handle a missing optional mount without an uncaught C++
+exception. The Android 12 vold AbortFuseConnections directory iterator is a
+known example where a missing mountpoint otherwise causes SIGABRT.
+
+A device FD still exposes its real driver's ioctl capabilities. Native
+file views do not promise to virtualize DRM/V4L2 hardware ioctl identity,
+CPU ISA/HWCAP, or privileged raw CPU instructions. Android SELinux remains
+responsible for app mount permissions. Enforcing, permissive and disabled
+modes require separate integration tests, as do Mesa and codec backends.
+
+Validation
+----------
+
+Build tools/testing/selftests/uts_views, then run uts_views as host root
+on a disposable test system with a writable unified cgroup hierarchy and
+cpu, memory, cpuset, pids and io delegated into it. The test uses separate
+PID/mount/UTS/net/time namespaces, two memory limits, fixed FDs, remounts,
+PSI triggers, descendant netns and init-exit invalidation. If card0 exists,
+it also validates an authorized DRM projection and standard coldboot.
+An optional first argument names an ARM32 helper inherited with UTS FD 9;
+this permits native/compat ioctl validation on an ARM64 kernel. Tests need
+CAP_SYS_ADMIN and CAP_SYS_TIME in the host and deliberately create load in
+their temporary cgroups. A missing kernel extension returns kselftest SKIP.
+
+These checks do not certify complete Android boot, SELinux policy labels,
+LMKD pressure handling, GPU/codec compatibility or isolation performance.
+Those require the target board and the final launcher/image integration.
+
+For one consolidated target-board acceptance pass, see
+:doc:`uts-views-board`.
